1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,111
Your story has people in it now, and calls, and answers that start jobs.

2
00:00:06,011 --> 00:00:08,570
It's getting hard to see all of it at once.

3
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:13,929
So today you don't learn a new kind of record.

4
00:00:14,379 --> 00:00:17,309
You learn four ways to look at the ones you have,

5
00:00:17,759 --> 00:00:21,233
and one habit that lets the tool write your to-do list for you.

6
00:00:22,433 --> 00:00:26,184
Thirty-one records, and twelve places where one of them points at another.

7
00:00:27,084 --> 00:00:30,112
You can't hold all of that in your head any more,

8
00:00:30,562 --> 00:00:32,675
and it's only going to grow.

9
00:00:34,475 --> 00:00:36,861
So I'll ask the tool a question.

10
00:00:37,761 --> 00:00:40,831
It's lint, with one more word on the end: missing.

11
00:00:41,731 --> 00:00:43,792
And it says nothing's missing,

12
00:00:44,242 --> 00:00:48,165
which means every pointer lands on a record that exists.

13
00:00:49,065 --> 00:00:50,384
Keep that answer in mind,

14
00:00:50,834 --> 00:00:54,586
because in a few minutes we're going to break it on purpose.

15
00:00:55,786 --> 00:00:59,693
This is the Quest Log on Helm's handheld, with First Contact open.

16
00:01:00,593 --> 00:01:03,016
The words in the pane are its description,

17
00:01:03,466 --> 00:01:06,627
written for the crew, and they tell them what to do.

18
00:01:07,527 --> 00:01:10,449
A description doesn't say why your story needs that step,

19
00:01:10,899 --> 00:01:12,348
and that's the job of a note.

20
00:01:14,148 --> 00:01:16,830
A note is one line, right under the heading,

21
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:19,782
that starts with an equals sign and a space.

22
00:01:20,682 --> 00:01:23,449
So this one says what the mystery is for,

23
00:01:23,899 --> 00:01:26,419
and this one marks the end of act one.

24
00:01:27,319 --> 00:01:28,941
The crew never sees a note,

25
00:01:29,391 --> 00:01:34,506
and First Contact's page in the Quest Log stays just as it was.

26
00:01:35,406 --> 00:01:37,895
You see it, though, every time you hold the mouse

27
00:01:38,345 --> 00:01:40,231
over a pointer to that record.

28
00:01:41,131 --> 00:01:44,116
And don't forget the space after the equals sign,

29
00:01:44,566 --> 00:01:47,119
because without it the line isn't a note at all.

30
00:01:48,319 --> 00:01:50,792
Now look at the top right corner of the editor,

31
00:01:51,242 --> 00:01:54,084
where the add-on has put a row of small icons.

32
00:01:54,984 --> 00:01:57,068
The first one is the outline:

33
00:01:57,518 --> 00:02:02,028
every record in a list, grouped by section, with a box to search in.

34
00:02:02,928 --> 00:02:04,935
The second is the timeline,

35
00:02:05,385 --> 00:02:07,238
and that's the one I use most.

36
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:10,534
It has columns called beats,

37
00:02:10,984 --> 00:02:15,747
and whatever's in beat two can't happen until something in beat one has.

38
00:02:17,547 --> 00:02:19,163
The third is the graph,

39
00:02:19,613 --> 00:02:23,088
which is a box for every record and an arrow for every pointer.

40
00:02:23,988 --> 00:02:26,390
All three redraw while you type,

41
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:29,204
and clicking a record takes you to it in the file.

42
00:02:30,404 --> 00:02:33,397
There's one thing you need to know before you trust it.

43
00:02:34,297 --> 00:02:36,312
The timeline follows a reveal,

44
00:02:36,763 --> 00:02:39,844
and it follows an answer that leads to another scene.

45
00:02:40,744 --> 00:02:43,815
But it doesn't follow a call that a quest places,

46
00:02:44,265 --> 00:02:46,900
and it doesn't follow what an answer starts.

47
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:50,644
So here's Tag the Hulk in beat one,

48
00:02:51,094 --> 00:02:54,813
even though that job only starts when Comms says, we'll tag her.

49
00:02:55,713 --> 00:02:58,107
Read the timeline for the chain of your quests,

50
00:02:58,557 --> 00:03:01,890
and for who calls whom, read the printed script, which is coming up.

51
00:03:03,090 --> 00:03:04,798
Here's the habit I want you to have.

52
00:03:05,698 --> 00:03:07,603
Act two is three ideas:

53
00:03:08,053 --> 00:03:10,929
the hulk is worth something, the crew owns her for an hour,

54
00:03:11,379 --> 00:03:14,965
and a Guild assessor called Vane wants to know her price.

55
00:03:15,865 --> 00:03:17,901
I'm not going to write any of that yet.

56
00:03:18,351 --> 00:03:21,577
I'm going to point at it, from things the crew already sees.

57
00:03:23,377 --> 00:03:27,373
This is Report to DS 1 in the Quest Log, on Helm's handheld.

58
00:03:28,273 --> 00:03:30,826
And this is Quill's second call on Comms,

59
00:03:31,276 --> 00:03:33,741
where Chief Ives asks for it on the record.

60
00:03:34,641 --> 00:03:37,950
It has one answer: it is ours, DS 1.

61
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:45,215
So in the file, this step now reveals a quest called claim, which doesn't exist.

62
00:03:46,115 --> 00:03:48,174
This sentence in its page has a link in it,

63
00:03:48,624 --> 00:03:50,764
two square brackets around a key,

64
00:03:51,214 --> 00:03:53,148
for a person who doesn't exist.

65
00:03:54,048 --> 00:03:58,002
And this new answer leads to a scene that doesn't exist either.

66
00:03:59,202 --> 00:04:01,387
Now I ask the same question as before.

67
00:04:02,287 --> 00:04:06,576
And this time I get three things, referenced but not written yet.

68
00:04:07,476 --> 00:04:09,552
For each one it tells me what's missing,

69
00:04:10,002 --> 00:04:12,635
which record points at it, and on which line.

70
00:04:14,435 --> 00:04:16,365
That's my writing list for act two,

71
00:04:16,815 --> 00:04:18,775
and I didn't have to keep it anywhere.

72
00:04:19,675 --> 00:04:22,680
The fourth icon in the editor shows the same list,

73
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:25,634
and clicking a row takes me to the line.

74
00:04:26,834 --> 00:04:28,840
So I write them one at a time,

75
00:04:29,290 --> 00:04:31,449
and ask again after each.

76
00:04:32,349 --> 00:04:36,526
The Claim is where act two starts, so it gets a page.

77
00:04:37,426 --> 00:04:41,229
Here it is in the Quest Log, on Helm's handheld, once it's written:

78
00:04:41,679 --> 00:04:44,951
the order, who else wants her, and what's at stake.

79
00:04:45,851 --> 00:04:49,061
I type it, I ask again, and there are two left.

80
00:04:50,861 --> 00:04:53,397
Next comes the person, Assessor Vane.

81
00:04:54,297 --> 00:04:56,904
He's the link in Report to DS 1,

82
00:04:57,354 --> 00:05:01,460
and once he's written, the Quest Log prints his name in that sentence.

83
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:04,572
Now there's only one thing left on the list.

84
00:05:05,472 --> 00:05:07,677
The last one is Quill's new scene,

85
00:05:08,127 --> 00:05:11,981
which plays on Comms when the crew picks the new answer.

86
00:05:12,881 --> 00:05:15,593
And with that, nothing's missing at all.

87
00:05:17,393 --> 00:05:19,512
Now let's go the other way, and cut something.

88
00:05:20,412 --> 00:05:22,499
When you cut a scene, don't delete it.

89
00:05:23,399 --> 00:05:26,221
Put a slash and a star on a line above it,

90
00:05:26,671 --> 00:05:29,471
and a star and a slash on a line below,

91
00:05:29,921 --> 00:05:32,396
and it's out of the mission and still in your file.

92
00:05:33,296 --> 00:05:35,025
Then ask the tool what's missing.

93
00:05:35,925 --> 00:05:38,755
One answer still leads to the scene I cut.

94
00:05:39,655 --> 00:05:42,586
It's this one on Comms, in Quill's first call:

95
00:05:43,036 --> 00:05:44,455
what do you know about her?

96
00:05:45,355 --> 00:05:48,128
If the crew picks it, the call just ends,

97
00:05:48,578 --> 00:05:50,226
and the story stops there.

98
00:05:51,126 --> 00:05:53,873
So a cut has two halves,

99
00:05:54,323 --> 00:05:56,267
and the list tells you the second one.

100
00:05:57,467 --> 00:05:59,563
In Class 1 you printed one document,

101
00:06:00,013 --> 00:06:02,102
and now you print all four.

102
00:06:03,002 --> 00:06:06,766
The prose one is your story as a book, with your notes beside it.

103
00:06:07,666 --> 00:06:09,785
The screenplay is every call,

104
00:06:10,235 --> 00:06:12,105
with who speaks, what they say,

105
00:06:12,555 --> 00:06:14,805
and what each answer does.

106
00:06:16,605 --> 00:06:18,082
That's the one to read aloud.

107
00:06:18,982 --> 00:06:23,362
But remember that where you wrote three takes, it prints three lines,

108
00:06:23,812 --> 00:06:26,441
and she only ever says one of them.

109
00:06:27,341 --> 00:06:29,900
The catalog is for looking things up,

110
00:06:30,350 --> 00:06:33,274
and the bible has the same beats as the timeline,

111
00:06:33,724 --> 00:06:35,062
with the same two gaps.

112
00:06:36,262 --> 00:06:39,588
And then you play it, because a list isn't a game.

113
00:06:40,488 --> 00:06:45,182
On Helm's handheld, Report to DS 1 is in the Quest Log,

114
00:06:45,632 --> 00:06:49,542
and the link in my sentence is printed as his name, Assessor Vane.

115
00:06:50,442 --> 00:06:53,789
On Comms, the second call has my new answer,

116
00:06:54,239 --> 00:06:56,272
and it leads to Quill's new scene,

117
00:06:56,722 --> 00:06:59,368
which has the answer that finishes the report.

118
00:07:01,168 --> 00:07:03,043
The Claim appears in the Quest Log,

119
00:07:03,493 --> 00:07:05,383
with Sable and Vane in its table,

120
00:07:05,833 --> 00:07:07,134
and a minute later it pays.

121
00:07:08,034 --> 00:07:10,114
Your notes aren't on any console.

122
00:07:10,564 --> 00:07:12,019
They were only ever for you.

123
00:07:12,919 --> 00:07:14,834
Next time we leave this mission for a while,

124
00:07:15,284 --> 00:07:16,412
and you write a boss.
